!SSHd - an SSH server for RISC OS 5
====================================

Double-click !SSHd: it puts an icon on the icon bar and listens on TCP
port 22, accepting public-key logins only.

Icon bar
--------
  Select  opens the log window (follows new lines, like tail -f)
  Adjust  opens the Keys window
  Menu    Info... / Log Window / Verbose (tick: more log detail) /
          Keys... / Options... (opens the settings file in your editor) /
          Quit
  Interactive help (the Help application) describes each icon.

Keys window
-----------
Shows the server's own host key at the top (generated on first run with
the CryptRandom module and kept in Choices:SSHd.hostkey), then three ways
to add an authorized key:
  - type or paste it into the field (the format ssh-keygen writes to
    id_ed25519.pub or id_rsa.pub), then Add or Return;
  - drop such a file onto the window;
  - Import: enter the account (user@host) and password of the machine
    whose keys you want to allow.  SSHd logs in there over SSH, shows the
    machine's host key fingerprint for you to confirm, reads
    ~/.ssh/id_*.pub and imports them.  The password is used once and not
    stored.  The desktop pauses while this runs.
Below that is the list of authorized keys, each with a Delete button.
Keys are stored in <Choices$Write>.SSHd.authkeys, one per line, and a key
that is already listed is not added twice.  The window opens by itself
when no key is configured yet.

Any authorized key has full control of the machine: every *command, and
over SFTP every filing system.  Only the user name "riscos" is accepted
(the "user" setting below); it is a filter, not a password: the key decides.

Use
---
  ssh riscos@riscos-host '*Cat'    run one command, output comes back
  ssh riscos@riscos-host           interactive * command line in a
                                   TaskWindow; Ctrl-D ends the session

Both single commands and
interactive sessions run in a TaskWindow child task (as used by Edit), so
they behave like a task window: line input, Escape via Ctrl-C, output as
it is produced, and the same limits for Wimp applications.  A command is
run from a small Obey file, so it may contain quotes and be up to 1000
characters (control characters are refused), and Obey replaces %0..%9
with nothing and %% with % before it runs; its exit status is 0 if it
completed and 1 if it raised a RISC OS error (printed in the output) or
was interrupted.

File transfer
-------------
sftp and scp work (scp in its modern SFTP mode; the old scp -O protocol
does not).  Clients start in the directory given with -r, by default the
boot disc, and see RISC OS paths the UnixLib way: HostFS::HostFS.$.Public
is /HostFS::HostFS.$/Public, and dir.file/txt is /dir/file.txt.
Filetypes follow the HostFS convention: a name ending in ,xxx names the
file without the suffix and sets type xxx on upload or rename (put prog
prog,ffb stores a BASIC file "prog"); otherwise the extension is looked up
with MimeMap (notes.txt becomes Text).  Listings show ,xxx on files whose
type the extension does not imply, and get prog,ffb fetches prog.

