Settings
--------
The first run writes <Choices$Write>.SSHd.Options (Options... on the icon
bar menu opens it), a text file with every setting as a comment showing
its default; remove the # in front of a setting to change it.  A line is
"name value"; anything from # to the end of the line is a comment.  The
file survives package upgrades, which replace !Run.  Settings take effect
when SSHd is next started.
  port 22            TCP port to listen on
  bind 0.0.0.0       address to listen on (all interfaces)
  root <Boot$Dir>.^  directory SFTP clients start in
  user riscos        the one user name that may log in; empty for any
  shell on           off refuses interactive sessions
  exec on            off refuses single commands (ssh host '*cmd')
  sftp on            off refuses SFTP and scp
  auth-timeout 60    seconds a connection has to log in; 0 unlimited
  idle-timeout 120   seconds a logged-in connection may sit with no
                     session open; 0 unlimited
  max-sessions 8     connections held at once
  banner on          send <Choices$Write>.SSHd.Banner (a text file you
                     create) to clients before they log in
  motd on            print <Choices$Write>.SSHd.Motd when a shell starts
  filetype ffd       type for uploads with no ,xxx suffix and no MimeMap
                     match (ffd Data, fff Text)
  verbose 0          1 for more log lines, 2 for SSH protocol detail
  log on             off stops writing <Choices$Write>.SSHd.log
  log-size 256       start the log file afresh past this many KB
The log file keeps the same lines as the log window; it is where to look
after a crash.

Command line options override the file (run sshd by hand in a TaskWindow
with -t for text mode): -p port, -d config-dir, -r sftp-dir, -b address,
-t, -v.

If the server cannot start (no CryptRandom module, port in use), the icon
stays on the bar, the log window opens with the reason, and Quit removes
it; nothing blocks a boot sequence that runs !SSHd.

After upgrading the package, Quit the running !SSHd and run the new one;
the Info window shows which version is running.
